WEBVTT

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.880
No. All my vacation, I've been putting myself out for that fish, Jerry.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.200
And if he wants to see me, he knows where I am.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.740
I'm dropping you a line, and I want an answer.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.560
I got them, Jerry. I got them. I got them.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.010
There are those fish sincere.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.450
Just worm snatchers.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.320
Come on, we're just trying to catch that 7 o'clock train for New York.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.070
What? Go back for my vacation without a fish?

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.560
Don't be silly. Please pass the worms.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.100
Well, if we're not on the job at the M.J. Malone company in the morning,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.880
we won't have any jobs to be on.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.820
I got a hunch this little worm is going to turn the trick.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:58.320
Yeah?

00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:58.960
Yep.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.160
I think I can depend on him.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:15.280
Look at him, Jerry. Look at him.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.380
Pull him in.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.080
Wait a minute. Are you pulling for me or the fish?

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.780
I got him. I got him, Jerry. Look at him.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.590
I got him. Oh boy, he's slippery.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.450
Smart, weren't you?

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:29.960
Wipe that smile off.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.830
Hey, how do you take these things off the hook?

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.480
I don't know. It's out of my line.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.370
Maybe you can find out up at the hotel.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.850
Oh, no. No, you can get hooked at that hotel, but not unhooked.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:43.910
Oh, come on.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.520
What? Go?

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.340
Oh, shucks. All right.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:10.620
Play football, Mr. Jordan?

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:11.740
Football?

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:13.810
No, no, indeed.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.330
This is the Tad Jordan unsinkable sports suit.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.750
Your money back if you drown while wearing it.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.330
What I want to know is, will it work?

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.690
I've often wondered.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:27.360
Now, I'm going to let one of you girls be the first to try it.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:28.660
Not me, brother.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.740
Not me.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.820
You try it. You'll go down in history.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.450
You'll never get me to go down in history in an outfit like that.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.130
I'll be seeing you. Goodbye.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.010
Goodbye. No matter.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.900
What was the matter with you on that last lap, Briggs?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:06.320
Nothing.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.160
Didn't you open her up?

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:07.760
Yes, sir.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.880
There's something wrong with that motor, Briggs, and I want you to find out just what it is.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:11.540
Yes.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.660
Oh, hello there, Colgate.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.660
This is a surprise.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.120
There's nothing to be surprised at, Mr. Rollins.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.500
Everybody seems to be here for the big race.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.020
I thought you got all your recreation in Wall Street.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.750
I never knew you to bother about boats before.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.030
Think you'll win the race, Rollins?

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:29.310
I'll win.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.300
If Briggs ever gets this boat tuned up right.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.050
Well, there's the Lackawanna Lodge.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.190
How would you Lackawanna over and get a room?

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.090
They say that's the swellest place around here.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.960
Hey, if you want to see something swell, take a good look at my feet.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.480
Well, you can soak them in hot water when we get to the hotel.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.460
That's no place for a couple of $35 a week brokers' clerks.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.480
Oh, come on. It's only for one night.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:58.670
All right.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:18.250
Here we are.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.420
Well, you go in and see if you can get a nice room in the attic.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:22.620
All right.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.090
Get back, sweetheart, get back.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.470
It'll cost at least five fish just to walk up on that porch.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:42.080
There, you stick your fish in my face.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.400
I beg your pardon, but you stuck your face in my fish.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:49.290
I guess you don't know what they do to you up here if you catch an undersized fish.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.290
Undersized, please, don't insult this animal.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.040
Why, you're liable to a fine of $500.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.570
You mean to say that I caught a fish worth $500?

00:05:59.370 --> 00:05:59.970
And that is...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.890
They can put you in prison for three months as well.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.310
Just what is the age of consent for fishes around here?

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.970
Look out, here comes the sheriff. You better hide that fish.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.640
Everything all right around here, Miss Parker?

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.650
Yes, except I lost my cigarette lighter.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:27.560
Oh.

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:36.270
Crickets are pretty bad around here.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:37.590
You been fishing?

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.930
Fishing? Of course not.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.740
Then what are you doing with that pole?

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.750
Pole? She's no pole. She's an American girl.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.380
Don't try to kid me, young fella.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:53.810
Why, Cheryl.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.160
Oh, I guess I left the lighter in my room after all.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:05.470
Oh, look. Isn't that your cigarette lighter over there?

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:10.560
There.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:13.910
Oh, I don't see it.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.070
I'm sorry. I see now it was just a couple of fireflies necking.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:22.630
You lose something, too?

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:23.640
No, sir.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.850
But I just seen a fella catching an undersized fish

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.230
out of the United States of America fish hatchery.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.500
Huh? What sort of looking fellow?

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.810
I couldn't see his face, but I seen his pants,

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.210
and I'd know them pants anyway.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:42.720
Yeah? Well, I met a lot of funny looking pants

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.740
down at the boat landing.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.410
Well, I'd take a look down there.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.240
Well, bonsoir, as them French Canucks say.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.270
Bon ami and a lot of lucks.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:58.080
They only had one room left, and they wanted $25 a day.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:07:59.980
They still have one.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:07.320
room left. Yeah, but I engaged the room. What? Are you crazy? We can sleep on a pool table

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.900
for 50 cents an hour. For once in our lives, let's see what it feels like to have the best

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:17.000
of everything. Eat real food and meet real people. Instead of that boarding house bunch

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.500
we just left up at Golden Point. Don't be ungrateful. Old Mrs. Golden was very nice

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:26.930
to you. She took you to her bosom. And now you say that the place was a bust.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:37.640
What time does the bull fight start can I take your bag sir? No come on take a chance. Don't be a piker

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:42.700
All right, and get us a couple of cigars. Yes, sir. What kind sir?

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:47.960
I'll show you that I'm no piker bring us two of your best 50 cent cigars

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:57.270
I'm sorry, sir. The cheapest cigar we have is a dollar all right and bring us one cigar and a good sharp knife. Yes, sir

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:06.380
Well, as long as we're going to stay here, we might as well go up and give the ladies a chance to look me over.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:08.500
Bonsoir.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.180
Hey, what do you mean by putting that fish in my pocket?

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:24.400
Well, the cats in the place were following me around.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.980
Well, not every girl can get a lot of cats to follow her around.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.980
He put a fish in my pocket and I had to go up to my...

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:38.320
change all my clothes. Have you got a suite? Of course. Well, then I'll meet you in the suite

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:57.520
by and by. Yes, yes. Then the board of directors offered me the presidency of the Drovers and

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:59.980
Butchers National Bank, but I had to decline because...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.100
because I'm strictly a vegetarian.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:03.090
Really?

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.200
The next highlight in my career was when Jerry Brooks and I

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.440
engineered the gigantic merger between

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.440
the reversible doorknob company

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.670
and the American Mustache Cup Limited.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:16.790
Where's Mr. Brooks now?

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:20.050
We matched for a million dollars and he won.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.640
He's out on the brand now counting his money.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:43.780
What a million dollars?

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.060
Elmer Peters told us you just won a million dollars from him.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.280
He did, did he?

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:51.870
Well, how have you been?

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:58.920
Mr. Brooks, what kind of a girl could you fall in love with?

00:10:59.120 --> 00:10:59.980
Well, uh...

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:06.620
I don't know yet, but just as soon as I meet her, a smash-up!

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:41.220
Is she badly hurt?

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.120
I think she's just fainted from the shock.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:46.150
Isn't she pretty?

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:49.590
She's lovely.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:53.730
Speak to me.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:54.900
Speak to me.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:11:59.380
Hey.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.060
I'll let go of your hip when you let go of my neck.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:16.600
Car, where's Babs?

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.520
Please don't get up. It's bad to get up too soon.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:22.860
I must see Babs. Is she hurt?

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.880
Your friend is all right. My pal is taking care of her.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.720
Why am I? That's all the sympathy I get.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:31.480
How do you feel?

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.070
She feels good to me.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:34.370
Are there any bones broken?

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:36.820
Nope, I've been over every one of them.

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:40.840
You know, that's a dangerous curve back there.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:42.660
Back where?

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.280
Say, I'm getting well enough to notice

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.440
that you're pretty darn fresh.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:50.200
Is Ips, my chauffeur, all right?

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:53.450
Yes, he's back there trying to figure out which is the best.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.760
Take the car out of the tree or the tree out of the car.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:12:59.580
I feel...

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:16.260
going inside. Coming Virginia? Sure. Did you carry me all the way in from the car? Yes. And I wish

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:29.860
it had been twice as far. Should you let me take the weight off your feet?

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:38.800
Yes, I'm afraid there is something broken.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:40.550
Oh, no, don't tell me that.

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:42.350
What is it? Look and see, quick.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.920
It's a strap across the back that seems to hold something up in the front.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:51.840
I'll take her, thank you.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.470
I'll double that, Si.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:09.440
Redouble.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.670
So your father is John Rollins, the owner of the Rollins Marine Engine?

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:21.210
Yeah.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.870
I wish you would stay at the lodge for just 24 hours.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:27.710
Why just 24 hours?

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.970
Well, that's as long as I can...

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.920
Well, that's a terribly short time, isn't it?

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.430
Not if you make the most of it.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:38.840
Why, Tad Jordan, what are you doing here?

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:43.150
I came up to sell your father mine unsinkable, uh, to meet your father.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:44.510
Well, meet Gerald Brooks.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.220
I hate to tell you, Virginia, but your chauffeur says your deferential is busted.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.020
Oh, does that mean there's something wrong?

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.730
It means that your car can't go to your father's camp tonight.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.510
So I'm gonna drive you and Babs up in my car.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.980
Oh, that's awfully sweet of you, Tad.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:05.330
but I'm going to phone father that I'm staying here tonight oh you think you

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:09.520
could get us a room well the lodge is pretty crowded but I'll try oh you're a

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:14.210
darling try and get us a nice outside room yeah I think I can promise it'll

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:26.800
be outside happy to have met you Brooks funny thing chances there was I driving

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:29.850
past the lodge no thought of stopping and then we get

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:35.090
tree there'll be a brass tablet nailed on that tree someday marking the historic

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:40.510
spot where Virginia was bowled over oh no marking the historic spot where Jerry

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:41.370
was bowled over

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:00.890
Great.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:41.490
And she says, looking for the love lie in the dark.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:04.940
Looking for the love light in the dark.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:01.840
but I must go back to Wall Street.

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:03.940
Well, I suppose you do love your business.

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:06.330
Yes, yes, it's a thrilling game.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:08.950
Millions pass through my hands every day.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:18.870
Gee, I wish I could meet one man who wasn't interested in making money.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:19.750
What?

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:23.380
Yes, I guess I'm going to have a hard time finding a husband.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:24.460
Why?

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.090
Well, you see, the trouble is I'm terribly, terribly rich.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:29.880
You are?

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.520
Why not let me share your troubles?

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:34.900
Oh, it's ridiculous.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:40.360
But most men seem to have the idea that they ought to have as much money as the girl they're going to marry.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:47.000
How much money would your husband have to have if he had this ridiculous idea?

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:54.640
Oh, it would practically limit me to a choice between Henry Ford, J.P. Morgan, or John D. Rockefeller.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.600
You're in an awful fix. Those men all have wives already.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:18:59.850
Oh, it's terrible.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.960
But with all my money, it would be absurd for my husband to go to work.

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.730
Do you mind talking in this ear? The other one is full.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.460
Oh, I want my husband to devote all of his time and energy to me.

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:17.870
Let me get this straight. You want a man with lots of time,

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:20.640
no money and plenty of energy. Is that right?

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:21.340
Exactly.

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:24.540
What you want is a high-class hobo.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:29.970
No, I just want a man who's willing to retire as soon as...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:30.960
as soon as we get married.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:33.090
Even if it's a noon wedding?

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:34.440
Oh, Elmer.

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:38.730
Babs, as I was saying, I'm crazy about you.

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.930
And please don't think that the money's got anything to do with it.

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:45.740
In fact, I'd forgotten whether you said you had money or not.

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:47.870
I've got oodles and oodles.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:52.070
I'd think just as much of you if you only had one oodle.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:59.970
Oh, Elmer, you know, you mustn't put your arm around me like that.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:03.680
When a man advances, it's better to give than to receive.

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:06.450
I know, but I can't stand it.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:07.740
You make me feel old.

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:09.920
Me too.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.520
What do you say you and I go around together?

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:13.710
Elmer.

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:40.940
what we are I wish that we could be like other very ordinary people we pay too much attention

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:49.500
to conventionality our social status has us up a tree but if you were just a traveling man

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:56.750
and not a millionaire I just a working girl who did

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:16.450
Bring me this wagon, so a chambermaid.

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:19.430
Say, when you buy me a lemonade,

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:29.820
set the trailsman for the chamber.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:48.820
Why should it don't mean a thing be hard to get?

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:02.430
of the chains for a maid.

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:09.710
Is it all right with your father if you stay here tonight?

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:11.590
Yes. Oh, Dad's a peach.

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:15.110
He's sorry you won't be down for the race on Thursday. Can't you say?

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:17.230
I'm afraid I have to go back tomorrow.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:20.380
Oh, I don't see why that old merger can't wait.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:22.140
What merger?

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:24.590
Well, the one that Elmer Peters told me about.

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.240
National dairy products and American waterworks.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:29.500
He told you that, did he?

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.940
But he said you'd probably be back after your conference with Mr. J.P. Morgan.

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:35.470
Morgan?

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:36.110
Yeah.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:39.560
Oh, yeah. Let's take a walk.

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:40.770
Oh, I wish.

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:04.710
When I was fishing at Palm Beach one time, I had quite an exciting experience.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:05.380
Really?

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.200
Yes, I hooked a dandy.

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:11.410
He put up such a fight that before he realized it, he had towed us halfway to Havana.

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:12.600
Well, Mr. Peters.

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:14.970
Oh, hello, Brooksie old boy.

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:17.980
You know the junior member of the firm, of course.

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:19.890
Go on, don't let me interrupt you.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:22.100
What did you do with this great tarpon?

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:24.160
Well, now let me think.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:27.640
Isn't that the one that I had stuffed and presented to the campfire girls?

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.100
Take it easy, take it easy.

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:34.660
This is your tie.

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:37.790
Girls, there isn't a room left.

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:41.570
Well, why not take our room?

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:42.880
We're in 62.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:44.350
That's a good idea.

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:45.690
I knew we'd lose that room.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:47.310
We only had it on margin.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:49.760
Where could you boys leave?

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.450
Why not right there, underneath your window?

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:56.600
The view would probably be good from there.

00:24:57.550 --> 00:24:58.210
Come along, girls.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:24:59.610
I'll show you to your room.

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:03.520
I want to thank you, Mr. Peters. You've been awfully kind.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:07.750
I try to be kind. I don't think we should leave at all for the Boy Scouts.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:08.930
Coming fast.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:11.420
She'll be along in a minute, I'm afraid.

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:15.410
Where are your suitcases?

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:18.400
We haven't any. We sent our trunks on ahead.

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:20.310
What? No pajamas?

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:21.540
Fresh.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:26.310
Ah, that's all right. You can sleep in my pajamas. I often do.

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:28.700
Too fresh?

00:25:47.690 --> 00:25:48.830
Well, here's our lower berth.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:50.680
Uh, stop squawking.

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:52.910
Yeah?

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:55.960
Fair sex.

00:25:58.870 --> 00:25:59.810
Is everything all right?

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:02.640
Just fine. Here's a blanket for you.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.010
Thanks. See you tomorrow.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:06.670
Good night.

00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:08.030
Good night.

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:11.350
Oh, Mr. Peters.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:12.190
Yes?

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:14.080
Here's yours.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.020
Will that be enough, honey?

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:20.990
Yes, I think this will about cover everything.

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:23.590
Is there anything else you'd like?

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:26.660
Nothing you can throw out the window.

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:30.460
Good night.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:38.920
Tuck me in and bring me a hot water bottle.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:40.030
Anything else?

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:42.720
Yeah, cuddle me.

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.350
Elmer, we're not going back to the office tomorrow.

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.760
I told Virginia we're going to stay on.

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.620
But what are we going to tell the boss?

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:54.660
At last you've got an opportunity to demonstrate your ability as a liar.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.530
But the trouble is the boss has such a suspicious nature

00:26:57.530 --> 00:26:59.870
he wouldn't believe a good lie if he heard one.

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:02.620
You go back, but I'm staying.

00:27:03.050 --> 00:27:05.170
Don't let your heart rule your head, Jerry.

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:07.960
Love is all right, but I'm old-fashioned.

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:09.460
I like to eat.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:11.830
No girl can make a monkey out of me.

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:13.780
I'm looking into the future.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:26.520
What are you doing?

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:28.790
Looking into the future.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:29.960
How does it look?

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.080
Look, it looks great.

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:34.860
Jerry, I'll stay with you.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:36.130
Good boy.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:47.400
Oh, that's all right, but it's cold out here.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:50.460
I can't sleep at all.

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:52.840
Why don't you count sheep?

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:54.770
How do you do it?

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.120
Well, you picture in your mind a hedge

00:27:58.330 --> 00:27:59.670
with a big hole in it.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:02.900
And up comes one sheep, and he jumps through.

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:06.260
And up comes another, and he jumps through.

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.440
And then a third, and he jumps through.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:11.320
Wait, wait, do goats count?

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:13.700
Yes, if it'll get you to sleep any quicker.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:15.760
All right, I'll try it.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:22.700
One, two, three.

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:24.960
Go on, go on, get away, get away.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:29.450
Four, five.

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:59.980
One million six hundred and eighty-three, one million six hundred and eighty-four, one million six hundred and eighty-five, one million six hundred and eighty-six, I'll never eat mutton again.

00:29:00.090 --> 00:29:01.830
Again, as long as I live.

00:29:03.740 --> 00:29:06.810
One million, six hundred and eighty-seven.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:07.930
How'd you make out?

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:12.350
Fine. I'm beginning to feel sleepy already.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:17.960
Oh, oh, oh, my back.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:24.100
Say, if you want to feel a good backache, feel mine.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:26.460
Well, at least we showed our chivalry.

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:29.980
Chivalry is right. I've been chivaling for a...

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:30.700
Morning.

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:38.990
Up early.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:39.430
Yes.

00:29:39.910 --> 00:29:41.250
I just got a phone call from the camp.

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:42.740
Daddy's coming down in the boat.

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:44.530
Oh, I do want you to meet him.

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:45.700
See you later.

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:46.280
Where will you be?

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:47.380
On the boat landing.

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:48.380
All right.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:48.950
Goodbye.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:50.180
Goodbye.

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:06.390
Come on, Mr. Peters. We're going for a nice, refreshing plunge in the river.

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:10.230
What, in that ice water? Not me.

00:30:10.510 --> 00:30:13.790
Oh, don't be silly. They say the water is warmer than the air.

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:15.710
And I ought to have slept in the water.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.040
I brought your bathing suit. You can put it on here.

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:22.790
There's no one about yet. I'll wait for you down by the river.

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:26.820
All right. You go down and get in first and get the water warmed up.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:29.590
I'll be along later. All right.

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:32.500
if I don't get stuck in a snowdrift.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:42.870
Millionaires have that dog-eyed beef.

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:04.010
Hello. Good morning, Sheriff.

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:08.680
What are them pants doing here?

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:11.130
Watch. Just sitting around.

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:13.490
They belong to that fellow that was catching fish

00:31:13.490 --> 00:31:16.250
contrary to the United States of America fish hatchery law.

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:19.080
Don't happen to be yours.

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:21.820
Parrish the thought.

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:23.860
I wouldn't go shoplifting in those.

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:28.050
Yeah. Well, when I find the fellow that owns them,

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:29.970
I'll clap him into jail so quickly to make his...

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:37.840
I wonder how the jails are up there.

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:41.700
Briggs, I know there's something wrong with that motor.

00:31:42.170 --> 00:31:44.050
It's perfectly all right, Mr. Rollins.

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:47.020
Hello there.

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:52.150
Well, so you broke up my favorite car, eh?

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:54.580
I'm sorry, we skidded right into it.

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:57.340
Oh, this is Jerry Brooks. This is my dad.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:58.670
How do you do, Mr. Rollins?

00:31:58.710 --> 00:31:59.110
How did you do?

00:31:59.290 --> 00:31:59.970
Oh, he's guessing...

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:01.480
He's very excited about the races I am.

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:02.830
Do you know anything about boats?

00:32:03.010 --> 00:32:03.350
A little.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:05.110
He knows everything about them.

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:08.950
Mr. Peters told me all about those motorboat races you won on the French Riviera.

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:09.330
Oh.

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:10.940
And that race at Monte Carlo.

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:14.260
That time when you got out of the boat to fix the propeller right in the middle of the bay.

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:15.740
And then went on to win.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:18.870
Who told you all about that?

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:20.650
Mr. Peters, of course.

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:22.660
It wasn't for his friend, Mr. Peters.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:24.340
He'd let you think he was just a nobody.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.990
Well, I'm certainly glad we have an expert with us.

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:29.970
Well, I'm not an expert, Mr. Rollins, but I do know a little about motorboats.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:32.380
My uncle was a boatbuilder at City Island.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:34.890
He took me out when they were doing speed tests.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:38.120
Have a look at my motor. There's something wrong with it.

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:40.060
If there's any trouble, he'll find it.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:42.300
Jerry designed the Brooks airplane motor.

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:43.920
Mr. Peters told me so.

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:44.940
Oh, is that so?

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:47.220
Come down and have a look at it, won't you?

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:48.060
Come on, Jerry.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:52.820
Briggs, step out a moment and let this gentleman have a look, will you?

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:53.280
Yes.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:54.530
Step right in.

00:32:56.180 --> 00:32:56.920
Try it out, Jerry.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:58.180
You think you can handle it?

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.610
Yes, sir, I can handle it.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:00.480
Go ahead.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:01.560
Thanks, Dad.

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:03.980
All right, break.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:04.460
Shove off.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:34.970
You know that motor? Why couldn't you get that speed out of the brakes?

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:36.720
It's a mystery to me.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:39.820
Well, you're no longer a mystery to me.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:23.880
a stop at the hotel tailor shop have a pair of pants made in a hurry

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:34.800
I forgot my eye.

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:41.690
I can dive.

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:43.560
Watch this one.

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:48.960
Don't be lucky if I hit the lake.

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:28.260
Is there a life-saving station in this block?

00:35:28.810 --> 00:35:29.770
Oh, you're all right.

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:33.290
Oh my, but this river has a hard finish.

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:35.380
Was I going along?

00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:37.240
About two minutes, Captain.

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:42.090
This? I had a hard time getting it off the horse.

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:05.530
Come on, Elmer. Why won't you dance?

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:08.070
I can't dance. My knee's getting away.

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.840
Say, when I dance, I don't know I have knees.

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:13.480
If you danced with me, you'd know you had knees.

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:34.030
A new contortion for that portion.

00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:48.140
Ah, Rick ain't hard to do.

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:59.220
Knee, boy.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:59.860
Show me some more, I'm going for a random

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:08.510
I've never thought of them I can take a chance

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:21.120
He's the man I want to see.

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:23.250
Have you thought over my proposition, Mr. Rollins?

00:39:23.450 --> 00:39:25.730
No, no, I won't put a dime in that device

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:27.150
until you can prove to me that it works.

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:28.550
Then don't move from this spot.

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:29.970
I'll be back with the proof within...

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.600
Two hours. Now, don't go away.

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:50.880
Jerry, that was glorious. Won't Dad be tickling?

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:52.360
I guess we showed a little speed, all right.

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:53.920
I'll be up as soon as I tie the boat.

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:54.580
All right, hurry.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.540
By the way, I hear that your uncle is selling short on my marine engine stock.

00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:07.980
And I suppose if your boat wins that race tomorrow, your stock will go sky high.

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:10.880
Well, why shouldn't it? It'll prove the value of my engine, won't it?

00:40:11.240 --> 00:40:12.380
Why, Vincent Colgate.

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:15.490
Hello, gorgeous. Do I get a kiss or don't I?

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:16.810
A very sisterly one.

00:40:17.130 --> 00:40:18.010
I don't want that kind.

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:21.820
Oh, Dad, did you ever see anything like the way Jerry handles that boat?

00:40:22.450 --> 00:40:23.310
Perfectly marvelous.

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:25.840
Why, all he did was adjust the carburetor a little.

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:29.080
Say, Virginia, I've got an idea.

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:32.960
You know, I think that if I were to...

00:40:34.480 --> 00:40:37.480
Hey, you! Come here! Come here!

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:40.480
I got a job for you.

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:42.980
I don't need a job. I'm Mr. Rollins Chauffeur.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:45.440
So much the better. Now, this is a very simple job.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:46.960
All you gotta do is put on the flight.

00:40:49.850 --> 00:40:56.260
See, everything! Dad's gonna let you pilot the Virginia in the race tomorrow.

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:57.570
Do you mean it?

00:40:57.630 --> 00:40:58.390
It's all settled.

00:40:58.690 --> 00:40:59.980
Gee, that's good.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:06.260
of your father to trust me like this dad likes you tremendously i'm glad and how do you feel on

00:41:06.260 --> 00:41:14.360
the subject well i always try to agree with father whenever i can you know i wish i liked you

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:23.760
just as much as you like me don't you no i'm crazy about you do you really mean that you bet

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:29.970
i mean it oh but i forgot why what's the matter you're terrible

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:33.390
Why, you're rich, too. Mr. Peters told me.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:35.820
I know. You don't have to tell me what he said.

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:39.320
Oh, but what has your money or my money got to do with it anyway?

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:41.650
Money doesn't mean everything.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:45.550
No, I guess it doesn't, if you haven't something else, too.

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:48.880
You'll win tomorrow, Jerry. I know you will.

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:51.060
With you pulling for me, I can't lose.

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:53.000
But I'm not going to take any chances.

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:55.520
I'm going to give this boat a thorough going over right now.

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:56.720
There's still something wrong.

00:41:57.030 --> 00:41:59.720
All right. I'll meet you at the lodge. Goodbye.

00:42:06.880 --> 00:42:10.220
Wait a minute. Now, when I open my mouth to put the tube in,

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:13.490
how do I keep the water from going in?

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:15.830
Oh, now you're annoying me with details.

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:21.960
Well, Brooke, I didn't recognize you at the first glance.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:24.180
So you're gonna handle the boat, eh?

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:24.740
Yes.

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:27.760
I'm in a position to spoil your little game for you.

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:29.970
What do you think my little game in?

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:31.630
There's hardly any question about that.

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:33.930
You're in hopes of marrying a rich girl.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:37.320
And I think I could name the young lady without much difficulty.

00:42:37.730 --> 00:42:39.970
I haven't any idea of marrying any rich girl.

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:43.050
Oh, then I presume you have no objections to my telling the Rollins

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:45.920
that you're nothing more than a little broker's clerk.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:50.240
How sooner you let me tell them myself?

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:53.970
Now, listen, Brooks, I have no idea of telling them.

00:42:54.660 --> 00:42:56.620
If you are prepared to do a little favor for me.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:57.420
What is it?

00:42:57.580 --> 00:42:59.680
If Rollins' boat wins the race tomorrow...

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:01.300
I'm going to lose a lot of money.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:02.520
Oh, so that's it.

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:04.900
But if it loses, I'm going to make a lot.

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:06.750
Go on and tell them.

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:09.000
Now, listen to me, Brooks.

00:43:10.210 --> 00:43:13.220
If you throw the race, I'll give you $30,000.

00:43:16.300 --> 00:43:17.140
$30,000?

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:19.580
Yes, and if you don't take it, someone else will.

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:20.460
Someone else?

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:22.310
But I'm going to pilot the boat.

00:43:22.390 --> 00:43:24.050
Do you think that Rollins would let you pilot this boat

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:25.970
if he knew you were going around under false pretenses?

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:29.970
Do you think that any other mechanic he might get would refute?

00:43:30.350 --> 00:43:31.010
30,000?

00:43:32.440 --> 00:43:33.380
Don't be silly.

00:43:33.970 --> 00:43:36.940
Then it would make no difference in the race whether I accept or not.

00:43:37.100 --> 00:43:38.400
Virginia can't win anyway.

00:43:39.170 --> 00:43:41.470
Only the 30,000 might as well be yours.

00:43:42.830 --> 00:43:43.390
Think it over.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:48.140
And I won't say anything about your masquerade.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:00.880
I owe you 50 cents.

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:02.850
Yeah, so does Mr. Peters.

00:44:41.612 --> 00:44:42.932
Have you been stealing from the farm?

00:44:43.340 --> 00:44:45.740
Are you kidding me or insulting me?

00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:46.940
Then what's the meaning of this?

00:44:46.988 --> 00:44:48.988
Gerald Brooks or Elmer Peters, Lackawanna Lodge.

00:44:49.356 --> 00:44:53.736
Just discovered negotiable certificate for 300 shares U.S. steel delivered to you are missing.

00:44:54.284 --> 00:44:58.084
In due your unwarranted absences, we are preparing to take drastic steps.

00:44:58.316 --> 00:44:59.976
Signed, M.J. Malone and Company.

00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:02.010
That certificate came when we were leaving.

00:45:02.190 --> 00:45:02.790
I got it.

00:45:02.870 --> 00:45:03.690
Well, what did you do with it?

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:05.720
I put it in my pocket.

00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:06.400
What pocket?

00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:07.600
That's the question.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:08.400
Which pocket?

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:09.970
Have a piece of gum?

00:45:10.050 --> 00:45:10.270
No.

00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:13.200
How about an almanac?

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:17.540
I know. I remember. I put it in my hip pocket.

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:19.870
Those aren't your trousers, are they?

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:22.410
No, I got these when the tailor wasn't looking.

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:23.230
Well, where are yours?

00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:24.550
The sheriff's got them.

00:45:24.770 --> 00:45:26.090
Well, go get them back from him.

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:26.470
What?

00:45:26.710 --> 00:45:28.430
And do three months for catching a fish?

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:29.970
Ha, ha, ha. Don't be...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:30.600
silly.

00:45:31.436 --> 00:45:33.716
I think we'd better go back to New York and explain.

00:45:33.856 --> 00:45:34.436
I can't.

00:45:34.604 --> 00:45:36.484
I'm handling the Virginia in the race tomorrow.

00:45:36.876 --> 00:45:37.876
Your what?

00:45:38.636 --> 00:45:41.036
The Rollins think a lot of you, don't they?

00:45:41.196 --> 00:45:43.436
Yes, but they wouldn't if they knew what a fake guy was.

00:45:43.916 --> 00:45:44.476
A fake?

00:45:44.844 --> 00:45:45.204
How?

00:45:45.932 --> 00:45:47.312
Junior member of the firm.

00:45:47.692 --> 00:45:48.692
Society man.

00:45:49.196 --> 00:45:51.336
owner of speedboats at Monte Carlo.

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:54.220
Well, that's what you get for telling a lot of lies.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.120
Who is the senior partner in this firm of Gerald Brooks?

00:46:03.200 --> 00:46:04.160
Elmer Peters.

00:46:04.444 --> 00:46:05.444
Elmer Peters?

00:46:05.884 --> 00:46:07.244
That name sounds familiar, too.

00:46:07.484 --> 00:46:08.484
I should think it might.

00:46:08.828 --> 00:46:12.428
Elmer Peters is president of the New York Stock Exchange.

00:46:13.372 --> 00:46:13.852
Oh, Jerry.

00:46:14.332 --> 00:46:15.832
I want you to meet Vincent Colgate.

00:46:16.444 --> 00:46:17.944
Quite a surprise meeting you up here, Brooks.

00:46:18.812 --> 00:46:20.652
You chaps must do pretty well down in the street.

00:46:20.988 --> 00:46:22.168
Oh, do you two know each other?

00:46:22.336 --> 00:46:22.796
Yes.

00:46:23.484 --> 00:46:25.724
Brooks is an order clerk at the broker's where I do my trading.

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:27.620
An order clerk.

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:28.620
Oh.

00:46:28.668 --> 00:46:29.728
Have I spoken out of turn?

00:46:30.188 --> 00:46:30.448
No, no.

00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:31.460
That's all right.

00:46:32.268 --> 00:46:33.988
I'll wait for you in the lobby until you're...

00:46:34.124 --> 00:46:34.964
Disengage.

00:46:35.692 --> 00:46:36.392
So long, Brooks.

00:46:38.988 --> 00:46:39.708
Is this true?

00:46:40.876 --> 00:46:41.136
Yeah.

00:46:41.708 --> 00:46:43.708
But Elmer Peters...

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:46.780
He told a lot of lies thinking it would help me with you.

00:46:47.404 --> 00:46:48.684
And I guess I thought so, too.

00:46:49.612 --> 00:46:51.612
But I should have known that a fellow in my position...

00:46:52.172 --> 00:46:54.932
has no right to act as I have toward a girl like you.

00:46:55.148 --> 00:46:55.668
But, Jerry.

00:46:56.172 --> 00:46:57.012
Don't you love me?

00:46:57.012 --> 00:46:58.212
Of course I love you.

00:46:58.604 --> 00:46:59.984
But now that you know the truth...

00:47:00.120 --> 00:47:01.000
I thought you understood.

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:04.360
I didn't fall in love with the rich young Wall Street man.

00:47:04.700 --> 00:47:05.860
I fell in love with you.

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:08.170
Take this jacket off. Take it off.

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:10.170
I'll do this job myself.

00:47:10.530 --> 00:47:11.670
Come on, take this jacket off.

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:12.970
You're going to get mad now.

00:47:13.110 --> 00:47:13.830
You're going to get mad.

00:47:14.090 --> 00:47:14.970
I was hoping you would...

00:47:15.790 --> 00:47:16.230
I'm not afraid of anything.

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:17.730
You got me mad, and I can't stand for it.

00:47:17.870 --> 00:47:19.950
I thought you were a brave man.

00:47:20.050 --> 00:47:20.930
I'm not brave.

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:21.430
No.

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:22.630
I drive a car.

00:47:22.690 --> 00:47:23.350
You're not afraid of the lake.

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:25.150
You're not afraid of the fishes.

00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:26.810
I'm not afraid of nothing.

00:47:26.950 --> 00:47:27.770
Put that up there.

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:28.990
Put that up there.

00:47:34.780 --> 00:47:39.350
That's what he says, but he's such a liar. Anything I hate is a liar.

00:47:41.240 --> 00:47:44.570
Oh boy, I hope it doesn't fail.

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:48.990
What do you mean, fail? It won't fail. Give me an anchor.

00:47:49.370 --> 00:47:53.500
Put an anchor on my neck. I'll show you, boy. Put an anchor on my neck.

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:55.900
Put it on there, boy.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:59.600
You've got more confidence in that thing than I have.

00:47:59.700 --> 00:47:59.980
Okay.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:02.000
Confidence, confidence, confidence, confidence.

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:03.520
You don't know what confidence is.

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:04.780
You have no confidence.

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:06.260
I have confidence.

00:48:06.380 --> 00:48:07.060
I have confidence.

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:09.540
If I walk down the street, you can tell I got confidence.

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:10.040
Wait a minute.

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:11.240
I'm going to get a good start now.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:12.380
Comp three for me.

00:48:12.460 --> 00:48:12.760
All right.

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:14.440
No more than three.

00:48:14.540 --> 00:48:15.380
Only three now.

00:48:15.840 --> 00:48:16.200
Comp three.

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:16.600
Wait a minute.

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:17.780
Comp three, boy.

00:48:22.880 --> 00:48:23.560
Take my day.

00:48:23.680 --> 00:48:24.320
I'll tell you tomorrow.

00:48:28.390 --> 00:48:28.870
What's the matter?

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:36.500
if somebody calls me up.

00:49:07.130 --> 00:49:08.090
champion swimmer.

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:09.570
That's right, girls.

00:49:09.730 --> 00:49:10.210
Give it a layer.

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:13.970
How can you tell?

00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:15.790
Good.

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:18.900
And get some white rock and frank ice, and we'll have a party.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:21.380
I'll get a barrel to roll them on.

00:49:21.520 --> 00:49:23.120
Yeah, and don't stop to empty it.

00:49:23.850 --> 00:49:24.510
Oh, girls, hurry.

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:32.330
It must be an almanac.

00:49:32.620 --> 00:49:37.230
See, FF first aid, FF fish.

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:39.990
Hey, here's something interesting.

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:44.000
Did you know that each pair of herring has two million children a year,

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:46.720
and that the male herring wouldn't know one of them if we met them?

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:47.520
Oh, never mind.

00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:51.160
First aid first. Here we are.

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:55.080
Victim's clothing, and then cover with a liberal quantity of butter or lard.

00:49:55.630 --> 00:49:56.130
Get some butter.

00:49:56.230 --> 00:49:57.920
No, no, that's not doom.

00:49:58.390 --> 00:49:59.970
You're right. Here we are.

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:11.880
I wonder what good this is doing to him.

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:18.260
Ah, here we are, the barrel.

00:50:19.340 --> 00:50:20.500
Get Jordan rolling.

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:21.740
Hurry.

00:50:22.610 --> 00:50:23.780
Come on, Ips, give us a hand.

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:24.420
Come on.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:26.060
Lift him up, Rico.

00:50:26.800 --> 00:50:27.440
On the barrel.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:43.620
Oh, sure, too much confidence.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.850
Here's $15,000, and another $15,000 will be handed to you as soon as the race is over.

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:10.090
Are you ready to go through with our little understanding?

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:13.460
All right.

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:14.120
Good.

00:51:19.090 --> 00:51:19.450
It's nothing.

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:21.210
I was runner-up in the British Open.

00:51:21.820 --> 00:51:22.880
Fullback for two years.

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:26.240
One at Harvard, and one at Yale.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:29.890
I used to play polo with the Prince of Wales.

00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:34.990
Do you ever hear from him?

00:51:35.940 --> 00:51:37.840
Not lately. I lost his address.

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:39.280
Oh, that's too bad.

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:41.220
What do you say we sit down?

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:45.580
It must be marvelous to be such an athlete.

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:46.780
It is.

00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:25.080
Hey, what's it all about? I ain't got my glasses and I can't read without them.

00:52:30.600 --> 00:52:35.370
We're going to go hard on a couple of young fellows.

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:38.380
Mr. Peter, Brooks has been looking all over for you.

00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:40.760
Oh, I know. You owe me.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:45.080
Excuse me, there. Looks like another big murderer.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:48.380
That's all right, Albert. I'll be waiting for you.

00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:52.350
Well, what is that?

00:52:57.230 --> 00:52:58.450
Hey, is that real money?

00:52:58.610 --> 00:52:58.810
Yes.

00:52:59.360 --> 00:52:59.980
And I don't want to...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:01.380
to mention this money to anyone.

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:02.120
You understand?

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:04.100
Yeah, but where'd you get it?

00:53:04.220 --> 00:53:05.500
Never mind where I got it.

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:10.620
Dad's terribly nervous.

00:53:10.740 --> 00:53:12.260
He thinks you ought to be out tuning up the boat.

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:13.600
Yes, I guess he's right.

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:14.600
Are you coming, Elmer?

00:53:15.170 --> 00:53:16.770
No, I'm afraid of getting mixed up in the crowd

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:18.050
with all this money in my pocket.

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:19.410
Why, what money?

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:22.130
Oh, he's holding the stakes on a bet.

00:53:22.370 --> 00:53:22.690
Oh.

00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:25.070
You know, Virginia, this is the last time

00:53:25.070 --> 00:53:26.570
I see you before the race starts.

00:53:27.850 --> 00:53:28.490
Goodbye, Elmer.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:30.300
Bye.

00:53:39.160 --> 00:53:47.530
One, two, three, my boot.

00:53:49.510 --> 00:53:52.670
Fourteen, three.

00:53:52.990 --> 00:53:55.070
Dang, how'd you like to make it better in the race?

00:53:55.340 --> 00:53:56.860
All right, how much?

00:53:56.940 --> 00:53:57.980
Well, you name it.

00:53:58.370 --> 00:53:59.890
Go on, go on, go down here, Macho.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.370
How about five?

00:54:02.120 --> 00:54:02.760
You're on.

00:54:03.180 --> 00:54:04.040
Five grand.

00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:05.640
Five grand?

00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:06.580
Yeah.

00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:07.870
Isn't that what you meant?

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:09.890
Oh, I meant five dollars.

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:12.080
Five dollars.

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:15.220
That's well-bent for a big Wall Street man.

00:54:15.670 --> 00:54:17.730
I take it Mr. Peters is back in the Virginia.

00:54:17.990 --> 00:54:18.690
Sure I am.

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:20.500
Five thousand men.

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:25.570
Elmer, don't you know the Virginia can't lose?

00:54:25.830 --> 00:54:27.390
All right, if you say so.

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:29.140
You're on for five grand.

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:32.750
It's new money, so it'll have to be five baby grand.

00:54:32.930 --> 00:54:34.270
I'll hold the stakes for you.

00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:36.480
It's boring counting money.

00:54:37.060 --> 00:54:38.740
What a lot of money you're carrying.

00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:43.510
It's nothing. Only 15,000, a mere buck in the drobbet.

00:54:43.760 --> 00:54:45.300
How would you like to bet the rest of that money?

00:54:46.330 --> 00:54:47.530
Bet $10,000?

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:48.490
Will you do it?

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:50.190
Will he do it? No.

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:51.500
No.

00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:53.460
But he'll bet you $20,000.

00:54:54.780 --> 00:54:55.100
What?

00:54:55.450 --> 00:54:58.310
All right, we'll each put up $10,000 and an IOU.

00:54:58.730 --> 00:54:59.190
Well, come on.

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:02.770
So, I told all the mistakes.

00:55:03.640 --> 00:55:05.240
I know I shouldn't do this.

00:55:05.360 --> 00:55:08.440
Well, Mer, what's $20,000 to you?

00:55:08.620 --> 00:55:11.920
Yeah, sure. What's $20,000 to me?

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:15.240
His fingers won't snap in less than $100,000.

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:18.980
Well, here we are at the landing, folks.

00:55:19.580 --> 00:55:20.800
The boats are tuning up now.

00:55:21.440 --> 00:55:22.500
Gina just came out.

00:55:23.740 --> 00:55:25.260
Well, I'm off. Are you kidding?

00:55:26.090 --> 00:55:27.650
No, I'd sooner not watch it.

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:29.960
Oh, come on. Come on, Ted.

00:55:32.180 --> 00:55:34.900
I'm going to stay here among my souvenirs.

00:55:35.080 --> 00:55:36.060
Come on, Elmer.

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:46.460
It'll be a good lesson to that old Spencer Colgate

00:55:46.460 --> 00:55:48.020
if he loses $20,000.

00:55:48.600 --> 00:55:51.500
If I lose, it'll be a college education to me.

00:55:51.940 --> 00:55:53.360
Oh, Jerry, he looks adorable.

00:55:53.780 --> 00:55:55.100
Perhaps he won't seem so adorable

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:56.480
when he loses the race for your father.

00:55:57.120 --> 00:55:58.800
Who's going to lose whose race?

00:55:58.920 --> 00:55:59.960
Well, Jerry's not going to...

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:05.840
He isn't, eh? I just this moment learned that a certain group of men are paying him $30,000 to throw the race.

00:56:06.020 --> 00:56:06.320
Oh!

00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:13.240
Peters! Stand right where you are. I just found my glasses and I read that telegram.

00:56:13.580 --> 00:56:14.320
I want you.

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:15.840
Somebody wants us.

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:21.320
I believe Jerry would take a bribe.

00:56:21.740 --> 00:56:23.860
All right. Just watch the race and you'll see.

00:56:32.110 --> 00:56:32.770
It's all right.

00:56:34.900 --> 00:56:35.680
Whose room is this?

00:56:35.800 --> 00:56:36.680
It's Daisy's.

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:38.900
You know, it's in her roommate.

00:56:39.200 --> 00:56:40.360
Who's the roommate, a girl?

00:56:40.580 --> 00:56:41.680
Of course it's a girl.

00:56:42.040 --> 00:56:42.960
Well, goodbye, Elmer.

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:44.700
The sheriff will never look for you here.

00:56:44.820 --> 00:56:47.000
Oh, wait, you're not going to leave me here all alone, are you?

00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:47.680
I must.

00:56:48.080 --> 00:56:49.720
Why, if anybody found us in here together,

00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:51.400
there's no telling what they'd think.

00:56:51.580 --> 00:56:53.020
I don't care what they think,

00:56:53.610 --> 00:56:54.990
as long as there's no telling.

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:57.700
I think I hear voices.

00:56:57.920 --> 00:56:58.340
The sheriff.

00:56:58.720 --> 00:56:59.520
All is lost.

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:03.840
But where can I hide?

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:06.510
Do you think they're going to go in the bathroom?

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:22.680
I wanted to ask you about the race.

00:57:23.900 --> 00:57:26.000
Colgate told me that Virginia has no chance.

00:57:26.180 --> 00:57:26.880
It's all fixed.

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:30.770
Who's what?

00:57:32.870 --> 00:57:32.970
You?

00:57:33.350 --> 00:57:34.850
It sounded like a man's voice.

00:57:35.430 --> 00:57:36.470
I'm a little hoarse.

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:39.160
I hear your boyfriend bet a pile of money on the Virginia.

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:48.420
Well, if we're going down to the floor to watch the boat race, Daisy,

00:57:48.620 --> 00:57:49.900
we'd better get into our bathing suit.

00:57:50.950 --> 00:57:54.900
Oh, um, you're not going to unbreath here, are you?

00:57:55.120 --> 00:57:55.560
Why not?

00:57:55.740 --> 00:57:58.020
Where would we unbreath if not in our own bedroom?

00:57:58.480 --> 00:57:59.980
Well, as to that, I don't know, but...

00:58:01.240 --> 00:58:02.550
Cold? Nah.

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:05.900
Then you can dress in the bathroom.

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:06.940
Why should we?

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:12.020
Hey, will you look at all the bruises I got in that crowd down at the boathouse?

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:13.860
I've got one, too. Wait till you see it.

00:58:13.960 --> 00:58:17.760
Why don't you come with it, Bab? I've got an extra bathing suit.

00:58:18.180 --> 00:58:19.260
Uh-uh. Not me.

00:58:20.670 --> 00:58:21.490
Why don't you?

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:24.940
I became your funny-looking boyfriend.

00:58:25.240 --> 00:58:27.660
Oh, don't talk that way about dear little Elmer.

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:29.980
Why, an attractive girl like Bab should go...

00:58:30.280 --> 00:58:31.520
with an egg like that.

00:58:32.030 --> 00:58:33.010
I can't imagine.

00:58:34.860 --> 00:58:36.000
Oh, your suit's in here, Daisy.

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:36.880
Throw it out to me.

00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:37.920
I'll put it down out here.

00:58:38.310 --> 00:58:40.210
Bam, how do you like my new underwear?

00:58:40.560 --> 00:58:40.940
Oh, what?

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:42.070
Oh, yes, Daisy.

00:58:42.910 --> 00:58:43.190
It's well.

00:58:43.330 --> 00:58:43.990
It's beautiful.

00:58:44.390 --> 00:58:47.440
Oh, for that?

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:49.470
For that toy, my dog.

00:58:49.570 --> 00:58:49.990
Are you sure?

00:58:50.820 --> 00:58:51.260
Yes, it is.

00:58:51.380 --> 00:58:51.960
I'd better look.

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:58.150
Yes, the dog already just licked my arm.

00:58:58.970 --> 00:58:59.490
I'm already.

00:58:59.750 --> 00:58:59.970
Where's...

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:02.880
Come down to the boat.

00:59:03.420 --> 00:59:04.400
Stop it!

00:59:04.950 --> 00:59:07.640
Well, if you girls are going to the races, you'd better go.

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:09.760
Speaking of races, it reminds me.

00:59:10.000 --> 00:59:12.700
Have you heard about the newly married couple that went to the races?

00:59:13.100 --> 00:59:14.120
This is a scream.

00:59:16.550 --> 00:59:20.810
Beth, Beth, what's the end of that story?

00:59:21.350 --> 00:59:22.590
Never mind the story.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.710
What was the idea of you pulling up that cover

00:59:24.710 --> 00:59:26.650
every time I hung it over the foot of the bed?

00:59:26.810 --> 00:59:29.930
Oh, darling, how could I see you with that cover there?

00:59:31.500 --> 00:59:32.820
You weren't trying to see me.

00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:34.740
Oh, Babs.

00:59:35.210 --> 00:59:38.110
You don't think I wanted to look at those other girls, did you?

00:59:38.350 --> 00:59:41.170
Oh, no, no. Not much.

00:59:41.390 --> 00:59:44.550
Why, I'm so modest that I won't allow lamb chops on the table.

00:59:44.870 --> 00:59:46.890
Unless they've got paper panties on.

00:59:48.100 --> 00:59:51.240
The Sheriff, this is a pretty how have you been.

01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:12.210
They're under the bed.

01:00:17.470 --> 01:00:20.680
They're not here. I'm not going after all.

01:00:20.920 --> 01:00:21.120
Huh?

01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:23.620
Well, I'm going whether you are or not, Daisy.

01:00:28.370 --> 01:00:28.530
Ben!

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:39.900
She has a headache. She's trying to get some sleep.

01:00:40.220 --> 01:00:42.000
Hasn't she a room of her own to sleep in?

01:00:42.230 --> 01:00:43.010
Of course.

01:00:43.290 --> 01:00:47.160
Sure, but the electrician is fixing her life.

01:00:48.020 --> 01:00:48.700
Oh, all right.

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:51.630
Who cares if I lie down here with you, Eloise?

01:00:52.790 --> 01:00:54.430
Don't awaken her. She's sleeping.

01:00:54.890 --> 01:00:55.930
I'll be very quiet.

01:00:56.340 --> 01:00:58.100
And now if you'll excuse me, Babs, dear.

01:00:58.320 --> 01:00:59.840
I'm really very tired.

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:01.820
Please pull down the shade.

01:01:02.040 --> 01:01:06.860
I'm trying to go out.

01:01:14.140 --> 01:01:22.950
Oh, honey, what are you looking so gloomy about?

01:01:23.760 --> 01:01:27.380
Vincent just told me that Jerry has taken a $30,000 drive to throw the race.

01:01:28.100 --> 01:01:29.620
Oh, don't be ridiculous, Vincent.

01:01:30.030 --> 01:01:30.710
He's a rich man.

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:31.590
Oh, no, he isn't.

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:33.390
He's just a poor broker plan.

01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:35.940
Oh, I suppose I should have told you before.

01:01:36.140 --> 01:01:36.600
You knew it?

01:01:36.910 --> 01:01:38.110
I've known it all along.

01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:40.020
Well, why didn't you tell me before?

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:41.560
Do you mean to say that you would love me to this?

01:01:41.640 --> 01:01:42.560
Oh, I love him, Father.

01:02:02.870 --> 01:02:07.550
You and me both! Let's go!

01:02:07.850 --> 01:02:26.380
Are you sure that Jerry has taken a bribe?

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:27.040
Certainly.

01:02:28.940 --> 01:02:29.920
Dad, what are you going to do?

01:02:30.440 --> 01:02:31.860
I'm going to drag him off of that boat!

01:02:32.060 --> 01:02:57.680
He's falling behind! The Comet takes the lead!

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:36.200
Go ahead.

01:04:07.520 --> 01:04:08.740
The comet is widening the gap.

01:05:10.360 --> 01:05:11.820
There's no use watching it any longer.

01:05:13.150 --> 01:05:15.190
The result is a foregone conclusion.

01:05:15.640 --> 01:05:16.940
The comet can't lose.

01:05:26.520 --> 01:05:28.390
Your guess is as good as...

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:00.620
You

01:07:32.010 --> 01:07:32.190
Hey!

01:08:14.620 --> 01:08:21.150
See what he's doing? He's going to lose by a narrow margin so it won't look so bad.

01:08:21.250 --> 01:08:23.170
I don't believe it! Gary, get away!

01:09:32.840 --> 01:09:35.660
And I knew that Colgate would bribe any mechanic who piloted the boat.

01:09:36.120 --> 01:09:37.800
So I thought I'd pretend to be bribed.

01:09:38.020 --> 01:09:39.700
Oh, Jerry, you're wonderful.

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:41.880
Open up. Let me get through here.

01:09:42.880 --> 01:09:44.580
Try to break up this little jamboree,

01:09:44.600 --> 01:09:46.060
but you two will have to come along with me.

01:09:46.490 --> 01:09:47.110
What does he mean?

01:09:47.330 --> 01:09:50.130
Boss wired to have us pinch if we didn't return that certificate.

01:09:50.690 --> 01:09:51.570
Well, return it.

01:09:51.790 --> 01:09:53.510
Yeah, sure. It's all right.

01:09:54.730 --> 01:09:56.550
Remember? I know where it is. I got it.

01:09:57.180 --> 01:09:59.360
It's in the hip pocket of the pants and the suit.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:01.740
Those checkerboard pants?

01:10:02.020 --> 01:10:03.980
I threw them in the river. They sunk.

01:10:05.180 --> 01:10:08.420
And we're sunk. Come along. Peaceful night.

01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:09.990
Jerry, Jerry!

01:10:11.100 --> 01:10:14.070
I'm strong for you.

01:10:14.310 --> 01:10:16.330
Yes, but you won't be on the jury.

01:10:17.190 --> 01:10:20.660
Wait for me, dear. I'll be back in 15 or 20 years.

01:10:23.110 --> 01:10:23.550
Goodbye.

01:10:25.250 --> 01:10:26.240
Oh, look! Jerry!

01:10:26.620 --> 01:10:27.260
What is it?

01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:32.040
But I thought you had it in your hip pocket.

01:10:32.180 --> 01:10:35.640
I did, but my hips move around so much I can't be sure.

01:10:35.980 --> 01:10:38.140
Well, Sheriff, I think that rather fixes things up.

01:10:38.500 --> 01:10:39.620
I'm afraid it does.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:40.780
Oh, Virginia.

01:10:41.140 --> 01:10:41.800
Oh, Jerry.

01:10:42.140 --> 01:10:43.500
Oh, Elmer.

01:10:51.780 --> 01:10:52.960
It's all right.
